        <div class="subtitle">A Proposal to Revolutionize Daily Living for Individuals with Disabilities</div>
    </header>

    <div class="content">
        <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
        <p>This research project aims to develop an adaptive, AI-driven navigation system that bridges the gap between physical environments and the needs of people with mobility and sensory impairments. By leveraging real-time data and immersive technology, we intend to create a digital layer that translates complex urban landscapes into intuitive, accessible experiences.</p>

        <h2>Research Objectives</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Environmental Mapping:</strong> To create high-fidelity 3D models of public spaces that account for varying levels of accessibility.</li>
            <li><strong>Real-Time Adaptation:</strong> To utilize computer vision to detect obstacles, detect service animals, and identify safe pathways dynamically.</li>
            <li><strong>Personalized Interfaces:</strong> To tailor the user experience based on specific disability profiles, whether visual, auditory, or motor-based.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Key Innovation:</strong> Unlike existing solutions that focus solely on static maps, this project introduces a dynamic "safety constable" feature. This is an AI agent that acts as a virtual guardian, alerting users to potential hazards in real-time and guiding them away from danger zones, much like a vigilant constable ensuring public safety.
        </div>

        <h2>Methodology</h2>
        <p>The study will proceed in three phases over 18 months:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Phase 1: Data Collection.</strong> Gathering anonymized data from existing accessibility apps and conducting field surveys with community stakeholders.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase 2: Prototype Development.</strong> Building the core AR interface and integrating the safety monitoring algorithms.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase 3: Pilot Testing.</strong> Deploying the system in a controlled urban environment to gather feedback from users with diverse disabilities.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Expected Impact</h2>
        <p>By successfully implementing Project AURA, we anticipate a significant reduction in the isolation and frustration often felt by individuals with disabilities when navigating public spaces. The goal is to foster a more inclusive society where technology serves as a true equalizer.</p>

        <p>Furthermore, the project seeks to establish a collaborative framework involving urban planners, technologists, and disability advocates. We believe that by working together, we can build a future where the city is designed for everyone, not just the able-bodied.</p>
